WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
The world's first elevator shaft

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.740
was installed four years before
the elevator was invented,

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.230
which sounds ridiculous.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.010
But inside this building,

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.950
the Cooper Union Foundation Building
in New York City,

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.900
is the world's first elevator shaft,

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.140
the first time a building was constructed

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.990
to have a space for an
elevator top to bottom.

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:20.930
Because when this was being
designed in the 1850s,

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:23.470
the architect and owner
looked at New York City

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:26.460
just starting to sprawl
wider and rise higher

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.067
and they predicted,
"Someone is going to invent

00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:32.540
"a safe passenger elevator soon."

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.560
Four years later, Elisha Otis
did just that.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.310
But that prediction wasn't quite perfect.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.810
- I think the remarkable thing
about the Foundation Building

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:45.680
is that Peter Cooper first recognised
that he needed to build a building,

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.370
and then that he would figure out
what he wanted to do with it.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:52.340
There were principles
that he was committed to.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:55.730
One was free education to the
working-class people of New York.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:59.810
He established a free reading room
before there were public libraries;

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:05.540
also, the largest interior space
in New York City in which to hold

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.720
lectures and debates, also open for free
to the general public.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:15.920
Peter Cooper's vision was both for a very
radical, progressive institution,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:22.230
but he wanted embedded in the building
radical new technologies and ideas.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.250
So, by this time, by 1850,

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.080
New York City was still really, really
crowded and dense.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:33.230
But the grid of Manhattan
had already been laid out.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:35.490
The Manhattan grid was
established in 1811,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:39.500
so everyone in New York City understood
that New York City was going to grow,

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.960
and it was growing, in the middle
of the century, exponentially.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:50.040
In fact, the population of
Manhattan would peak in 1910,

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.980
and we still have not reached the density

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.780
and the high population that
the city had at that time.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:02.020
So, Peter Cooper knew that the city
had to be able to grow outward,

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:04.590
but it also had to be able to grow upward.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.360
And so, he knew the elevator was coming.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.770
At the same time, Elisha Otis,

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:13.350
who we all know from the
Otis Elevator Company,

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:17.480
was busy working on not
inventing an elevator,

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.130
because the elevator had
been in use for centuries

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:23.500
to move equipment, to move goods.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.830
What Otis understood was that elevators

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:31.650
would never be used for
people until they were safe.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:37.000
So, what he was working on doing
is to invent the safety elevator.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.780
That's happening in 1855.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.080
The Foundation Building
meanwhile is under construction.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.660
So, Peter Cooper knew this was coming.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:46.070
The only thing nobody knew

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:49.660
was what would be the
shape of the elevator cab,

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:52.970
and Peter Cooper bet on circular.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:56.350
Since it's the most efficient way,
spatially,

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:59.630
to put the most number of
people in the smaller shaft

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:01.830
and to carry them vertically.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:06.730
Little did he know that
that was not going to be

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:10.360
the conventional elevator cab shape.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:15.820
And so, this was the first elevator shaft
that was constructed, we think, worldwide,

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.440
certainly in the city,

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.650
but it was not the first
functioning elevator.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:25.700
And the elevator cab that was
ultimately put in this shaft was square,

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:29.840
so it was literally a
square peg in a round hole.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:36.470
The building was renovated, really,
from top to bottom on the inside in 1975.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:41.180
The architect, John Hejduk,
makes sure that the round shaft

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:44.000
is really floating in the space.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.360
And the other thing he does is
he makes a companion elevator

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.480
diagonally across the floor,
which is a square elevator.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:53.180
Always thought that
that was a beautiful way

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:56.670
to allow architecture
to speak across time.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.190
Peter Cooper's intuition
that the shape would be round

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:03.360
was not what the market
would carry forward.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.940
When you're thinking ahead of your time,

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:09.560
sometimes you will guess correctly
and sometimes you won't.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:14.360
But taking the risk to
actually invent something new

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.130
and to build it into a building, I think,

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.840
is really an innovative idea.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:20.940
- Is it worth the risk of
making that sort of prediction

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.980
when it is basically just a gamble?

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:26.400
Given what hassle a round
elevator shaft turned out to be,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.290
was it worth it?
Or would it have been better to wait

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:32.200
and then retrofit a
regular elevator in later,

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.560
like every other building from that era?

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.390
And here's a bigger question:

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.430
What should we be designing for now?

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:40.550
Not just in buildings, but anywhere.

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:42.730
What's the thing that
is going to seem obvious

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:46.037
in 20 or 30 years but no
one can see coming now?

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:49.003
I don't know.
Maybe you do.

